import torchaudio as ta
import torch
from chatterbox.tts_turbo import ChatterboxTurboTTS

# Load the Turbo model
model = ChatterboxTurboTTS.from_pretrained(device="cpu")

# Generate with Paralinguistic Tags
text = "DevOps is a way of working that brings software development and IT operations together, and today I want to explain what DevOps is, why companies use it, what kind of tools are used in DevOps, and what a DevOps engineer usually does in a company. First of all, DevOps is not only a job title, a programming language, or a single software tool. DevOps is mainly a culture and a set of practices that help development teams and operations teams work together more effectively. In the past, software developers usually wrote the code and then gave the application to the operations team. The operations team was responsible for installing the application, managing the servers, solving problems, and keeping the system online. Sometimes these two teams had different goals. Developers wanted to release new features quickly, while operations teams wanted the system to be stable and secure. Because of this, communication problems and delays could happen. DevOps tries to solve this problem by creating better communication, automation, and shared responsibility between these teams. One of the most important ideas in DevOps is automation. In a modern software project, many tasks can be repeated every day. For example, developers may need to build an application, run tests, create a new version, deploy the application to a server, check the system, and collect logs. If people do all of these tasks manually, it can take a lot of time and there is a higher chance of human error. DevOps engineers try to automate these processes as much as possible. This means that computers and scripts can do repetitive tasks automatically. One very common concept in DevOps is CI/CD. CI means Continuous Integration, and CD can mean Continuous Delivery or Continuous Deployment. Continuous Integration means that developers regularly add their code changes to a shared project. Every time new code is added, automated systems can build the application and run tests. For example, if a developer changes an important part of the application and accidentally creates a problem, the CI system can detect it early. Continuous Delivery means that the application is always ready to be released. Continuous Deployment goes one step further and can automatically release approved changes to production. Tools like Jenkins, GitHub Actions, GitLab CI, Azure DevOps, and CircleCI can be used for CI/CD processes. Another important part of DevOps is version control. Version control allows developers and engineers to track changes in code and configuration files. Git is the most common version control system today. Platforms such as GitHub, GitLab, and Bitbucket use Git and make teamwork easier. For example, if ten developers are working on the same project, Git helps them work on different features without destroying each other's work. They can create branches, review code, merge changes, and return to an older version when something goes wrong. Containers are also very important in DevOps. Docker is probably the most well-known container tool. A container packages an application together with the files and dependencies it needs to run. This is useful because sometimes an application works on a developer's computer but does not work on a server because the environments are different. With Docker, the development, testing, and production environments can be more similar. We can create a Docker image and run the same application in many different systems. When companies have many containers, managing them manually becomes difficult. This is where Kubernetes becomes useful. Kubernetes is a container orchestration platform. It can automatically manage, start, stop, restart, and scale containers. For example, imagine an online shopping website. On a normal day, maybe five application servers are enough, but during a big discount campaign, thousands of users may visit the website. Kubernetes can help increase the number of application instances when the traffic becomes high and reduce them later when the traffic goes down. Cloud computing is another major part of modern DevOps. Many companies use cloud providers instead of buying and managing all physical servers themselves. Some popular cloud platforms are Amazon Web Services, Microsoft Azure, and Google Cloud Platform. These cloud platforms provide virtual machines, databases, storage services, networking, security tools, monitoring systems, and many other services. A DevOps engineer may work with one or more of these platforms. For example, they may create virtual servers, configure networks, manage permissions, deploy applications, and monitor cloud resources. Another important concept is Infrastructure as Code, also called IaC. Normally, a system administrator can create servers, networks, and cloud resources by clicking buttons in a graphical interface. However, doing this manually can be slow and difficult to repeat. Infrastructure as Code means that infrastructure can be described using configuration files. Tools like Terraform and Ansible are very popular in this area. Terraform is often used to create infrastructure such as virtual machines, networks, cloud databases, and storage. Ansible is commonly used for configuration management and automation. For example, if a company needs to install the same software on one hundred servers, using Ansible can be much easier than connecting to every server one by one. Monitoring is also very important in DevOps because releasing an application is not the end of the work. After deployment, teams need to understand whether the application is working correctly. They need to monitor things like CPU usage, memory usage, disk space, network traffic, response time, and application errors. Tools such as Prometheus, Grafana, Datadog, Zabbix, and New Relic can be used for monitoring. Prometheus can collect system metrics, and Grafana can show these metrics in visual dashboards. For example, if CPU usage becomes very high or an application starts returning many errors, the monitoring system can send an alert to the engineering team. Logs are also important. Applications and servers create logs that show what is happening inside the system. When there is a problem, logs can help engineers understand the reason. Tools such as Elasticsearch, Logstash, and Kibana are often used together and are sometimes called the ELK Stack. Another popular option is Grafana Loki. DevOps also has a strong relationship with security. In older projects, security checks were sometimes done near the end of the development process. Today, many companies use the idea of DevSecOps. DevSecOps means adding security practices into the DevOps process from the beginning. For example, automated security tools can check source code, container images, dependencies, and cloud configurations. Security problems can then be found before the software reaches production. This is important because fixing a security problem early is usually easier and cheaper than fixing it after an attack. A DevOps engineer does not need to be an expert in every tool, but having a good understanding of Linux is very important. Many servers and cloud systems use Linux. A DevOps engineer should know basic Linux commands, file permissions, users, services, processes, networking, package management, and shell scripting. Bash scripting is very useful for automation. Python is also popular because it is easy to learn and can be used to create automation scripts, APIs, and system tools. Networking knowledge is also useful. A DevOps engineer should understand basic ideas such as IP addresses, DNS, ports, TCP and UDP, firewalls, HTTP, HTTPS, load balancers, and proxies. For example, if an application is running correctly on a server but users cannot access it, the problem may not be the application itself. The problem could be DNS, a firewall rule, a closed port, or a network configuration. DevOps engineers also work closely with databases. They usually do not need to be database administrators, but understanding basic SQL databases such as PostgreSQL and MySQL can be helpful. Knowledge about NoSQL databases such as MongoDB or Redis can also be useful depending on the project. Backups and disaster recovery are also important topics. Companies need to protect their data and be prepared for system failures. DevOps engineers can help create automated backups and recovery plans. Teamwork and communication are also very important in DevOps. A DevOps engineer often works with software developers, system administrators, cybersecurity teams, network engineers, testers, and project managers. Because of this, technical skills alone are not enough. Engineers should also be able to explain problems clearly, document their work, and communicate with different teams. DevOps is especially useful for companies that need to release software frequently. For example, large online services may release new versions many times in one day. Without automation, this would be very difficult. DevOps allows teams to make smaller changes more often instead of creating one very large update every few months. Smaller changes are usually easier to test and easier to fix if something goes wrong. There are also some challenges in DevOps. Learning many tools can feel difficult for beginners. There are tools for cloud systems, containers, CI/CD, monitoring, security, networking, infrastructure, and automation. However, beginners do not need to learn everything at the same time. A good learning path can start with Linux, Git, networking, and basic scripting. After that, a person can learn Docker, CI/CD, one cloud platform, Terraform, Ansible, and Kubernetes. It is better to understand the basic concepts first instead of only memorizing commands. A simple DevOps project can also help a lot. For example, a beginner can create a small web application, upload the code to GitHub, create a Docker container, use GitHub Actions to automatically build the application, deploy it to a cloud server, and add basic monitoring. This type of project can show how different DevOps tools work together. In the future, DevOps will probably continue to change because cloud computing, artificial intelligence, cybersecurity, and automation are developing quickly. Artificial intelligence can already help engineers analyze logs, detect unusual behavior, create scripts, and solve technical problems. However, engineers still need to understand the systems they manage. Automation is powerful, but if we automate a bad configuration, we can also create problems faster. Because of this, understanding the basic principles is very important. To summarize, DevOps is a culture and a technical approach that connects software development and IT operations. Its main goals are to improve communication, automate repetitive tasks, release software faster, increase reliability, and reduce errors. Some of the most common DevOps tools and technologies are Git, GitHub, Docker, Kubernetes, Jenkins, GitHub Actions, Terraform, Ansible, AWS, Azure, Google Cloud, Prometheus, Grafana, and Linux. A DevOps engineer works with many different technologies, but the most important skill is understanding how all these systems work together. DevOps is not simply about using many tools. It is about creating a better and more reliable software delivery process. For someone who enjoys software, systems, networking, cloud technologies, automation, and problem solving, DevOps can be a very interesting career path."

# Generate audio (requires a reference clip for voice cloning)
wav = model.generate(
    text,
    audio_prompt_path="kayitdeneme.wav",
    max_gen_len=2000,
    max_chunk_chars=250,
    chunk_pause_ms=150,
)
ta.save("test-turbo4.wav", wav, model.sr)
